            <ArticleTitle>Paraneoplastic syndrome demonstrated on (99m)Tc-HMDP bone scan.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2271-2272</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 23-year-old man, with no relevant medical history, presented with inflammatory peripheral and axial polyarthritis, wrist pain, and persistent low-grade fever for the past 4 months. A bone scintigraphy showed intense periosteal early and delayed uptake in long bones, with normal uptake in the spine, pelvis, and rib cage, and no clear focus of hypermetabolism. CT scan revealed a mediastinal mass. A biopsy of the mass demonstrated Hodgkin lymphoma with bulky disease. This paraneoplastic syndrome as the first sign of intrathoracic Hodgkin's disease is rare.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
